refore was a violation of u.s  sanctions against iran  still ahead the changes to copa 19  treatments that are helping patients and  those  on the front lines  you're watching global national  it's been nearly a year since the wh  first reported a mysterious  pneumonia-like illness  that was making people sick in china  ever since frontline healthcare workers  have been on a steep learning curve  battling an unknown and invisible enemy  as jeff semple reports from inside one  of toronto's hospitals  doctors are quickly developing novel  ways to fight back  alexandra brill was pregnant just days  away from her due date  when she tested positive for covid19  now i'm pregnant and i have covered and  i'm at the end of my term  and i'm about to give birth what's going  to happen during the first wave of the  pandemic abril's diagnosis would have  also  shaken hospital staff infected mothers  in some hospitals were kept away from  their newborns for up to two weeks  but babies have proved remarkably  resilient  overall outcome outcomes have looked  very good  abril's daughter was born on her final  day of quarantine and they were allowed  to go  home together the baby  tested negative i think as long as mom  uses the proper precautions  then you know keeping mom and baby  together are the most important  thing over the past 10 months staff at  this toronto hospital have had a crash  course in covid 19. we know how to treat  it better  dr jamie spiegelman works in the  intensive care unit which is nearing  capacity  half his patients have covered 19.  this 69 year old isn't getting enough  oxygen so the team performs a delicate  dance  carefully flipping him onto his stomach  increasing his chances of survival  patients also now receive steroids such  as dexamethasone  but despite all they've learned we don't  completely understand it and i don't  think we'll understand it until it's  over  doctors point to the unexpected case of  delroy noble  that's him back in may on a 